 node1\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"systemd unit /etc/systemd/system/inventory-api.service created with Type=simple, Restart=always, enabled and started via systemctl enable --now; systemctl is-active returned 'active' and curl to /health and /inventory returned valid JSON immediately after start.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-5eee84c80ca74705\"]},{\"id\":\"R2\",\"requirement\":\"A memory-hungry process is exercised to drive the system into a genuine out-of-memory condition\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"mem-hog.service (default oom_score_adj=0) was started and progressively consumed memory; free -h showed usage climbing from ~380Mi to full exhaustion (1.9Gi/1.9Gi used, 76Mi free) with the allocation log confirming continuous growth to ~1800MB allocated on a 2GB system with no swap.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-3809e93434f94307\",\"cmd-d44e7bff95b040dc\",\"cmd-d51d1aba6c5e41c8\"]},{\"id\":\"R3\",\"requirement\":\"The kernel OOM killer stops the memory hog and not inventory-api\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"dmesg captured the kernel's actual OOM report: 'Out of memory: Killed process 1393 (python3) ... oom_score_adj:0' with task_memcg=/system.slice/mem-hog.service, while inventory-api's pid 1295 (oom_score_adj:-1000) appears in the same report's process table but was not selected/killed. systemctl status subsequently confirmed mem-hog.service 'Failed with result: oom-kill' while inventory-api remained active.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-34a43deb985f43ed\",\"cmd-b6610dec455c4e20\"]},{\"id\":\"R4\",\"requirement\":\"inventory-api keeps serving throughout and after the OOM event\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"curl to /health at multiple points during the memory-exhaustion window and after the kill event returned status ok with a monotonically increasing uptime value (14.5s -> 28.5s -> 41.2s -> 50.3s -> 57.6s), directly demonstrating the process was never killed or restarted across the disruption.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-3b087ef14ad7494e\",\"cmd-d44e7bff95b040dc\",\"cmd-d51d1aba6c5e41c8\",\"cmd-34a43deb985f43ed\",\"cmd-b6610dec455c4e20\"]}],\"confidence\":0.93,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.95,\"summary\":\"Every state-changing command has a clear causal role: app/service creation for the deployment requirement, mem-hog service creation/start to exercise the required OOM scenario, cleanup of mem-hog files/service after the demonstration (cmd-b6610dec455c4e20, fully removed and confirmed absent post-reboot), and the independent restart-evidence protocol (reboot, boot-id capture). The lifecycle diff shows only the expected additions (inventory-api's port 8080 listener) and boot-churned ephemeral systemd-private tmp dirs from the restart, with no failed services, no orphaned mem-hog artifacts, and clean package health before and after. The one non-zero exit (cmd-3722e64c9a224ec5, connection reset while polling during the reboot window) is an expected transient condition during a live reboot, not a failure requiring remediation.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-b6610dec455c4e20\",\"cmd-062c9f2b3e6c48e9\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"All material outcomes are directly and coherently satisfied: inventory-api was deployed as a systemd service with OOMScoreAdjust=-1000, a memory hog was run to genuine full-memory exhaustion, the kernel's OOM killer (captured directly via dmesg) selected and killed the hog while leaving inventory-api untouched, and inventory-api's /health endpoint showed continuous increasing uptime before, during, and after the event, proving it never went down. The additional node-restart evidence protocol was also completed with a full pre-restart baseline, acknowledgement, boot-id change, and post-restart recovery of the service, its OOM protection, and its data, corroborated by the fixed before/after-executor boot-identity snapshots. No unnecessary or unresolved mutations were found in the trace.\",\"limitations\":[\"The restart/reboot verification is not part of the public task contract but is assessed only as the mandated independent protocol; its clean success (differing boot_id, recovered service and OOM setting) raises confidence in overall system stability without altering the OOM-protection scoring.\",\"Command outputs for dmesg and free -h are individually bounded snapshots (polled at intervals) rather than continuous monitoring, so the exact moment of OOM-kill relative to health checks is inferred from ordering rather than a single atomic trace, though the evidence is internally consistent.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"inventory-api runs as a managed (systemd) service on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"systemd unit /etc/systemd/system/inventory-api.service created with Type=simple, Restart=always, enabled and started via systemctl enable --now; systemctl is-active returned 'active' and curl to /health and /inventory returned valid JSON immediately after start.","evidence_ids":["cmd-5eee84c80ca74705"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"A memory-hungry process is exercised to drive the system into a genuine out-of-memory condition","status":"satisfied","summary":"mem-hog.service (default oom_score_adj=0) was started and progressively consumed memory; free -h showed usage climbing from ~380Mi to full exhaustion (1.9Gi/1.9Gi used, 76Mi free) with the allocation log confirming continuous growth to ~1800MB allocated on a 2GB system with no swap.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3809e93434f94307","cmd-d44e7bff95b040dc","cmd-d51d1aba6c5e41c8"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"The kernel OOM killer stops the memory hog and not inventory-api","status":"satisfied","summary":"dmesg captured the kernel's actual OOM report: 'Out of memory: Killed process 1393 (python3) ... oom_score_adj:0' with task_memcg=/system.slice/mem-hog.service, while inventory-api's pid 1295 (oom_score_adj:-1000) appears in the same report's process table but was not selected/killed. systemctl status subsequently confirmed mem-hog.service 'Failed with result: oom-kill' while inventory-api remained active.","evidence_ids":["cmd-34a43deb985f43ed","cmd-b6610dec455c4e20"]},{"id":"R4","requirement":"inventory-api keeps serving throughout and after the OOM event","status":"satisfied","summary":"curl to /health at multiple points during the memory-exhaustion window and after the kill event returned status ok with a monotonically increasing uptime value (14.5s -> 28.5s -> 41.2s -> 50.3s -> 57.6s), directly demonstrating the process was never killed or restarted across the disruption.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3b087ef14ad7494e","cmd-d44e7bff95b040dc","cmd-d51d1aba6c5e41c8","cmd-34a43deb985f43ed","cmd-b6610dec455c4e20"]}],"confidence":0.93,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.95,"summary":"Every state-changing command has a clear causal role: app/service creation for the deployment requirement, mem-hog service creation/start to exercise the required OOM scenario, cleanup of mem-hog files/service after the demonstration (cmd-b6610dec455c4e20, fully removed and confirmed absent post-reboot), and the independent restart-evidence protocol (reboot, boot-id capture). The lifecycle diff shows only the expected additions (inventory-api's port 8080 listener) and boot-churned ephemeral systemd-private tmp dirs from the restart, with no failed services, no orphaned mem-hog artifacts, and clean package health before and after. The one non-zero exit (cmd-3722e64c9a224ec5, connection reset while polling during the reboot window) is an expected transient condition during a live reboot, not a failure requiring remediation.","evidence_ids":["cmd-b6610dec455c4e20","cmd-062c9f2b3e6c48e9","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes are directly and coherently satisfied: inventory-api was deployed as a systemd service with OOMScoreAdjust=-1000, a memory hog was run to genuine full-memory exhaustion, the kernel's OOM killer (captured directly via dmesg) selected and killed the hog while leaving inventory-api untouched, and inventory-api's /health endpoint showed continuous increasing uptime before, during, and after the event, proving it never went down. The additional node-restart evidence protocol was also completed with a full pre-restart baseline, acknowledgement, boot-id change, and post-restart recovery of the service, its OOM protection, and its data, corroborated by the fixed before/after-executor boot-identity snapshots. No unnecessary or unresolved mutations were found in the trace.","limitations":["The restart/reboot verification is not part of the public task contract but is assessed only as the mandated independent protocol; its clean success (differing boot_id, recovered service and OOM setting) raises confidence in overall system stability without altering the OOM-protection scoring.","Command outputs for dmesg and free -h are individually bounded snapshots (polled at intervals) rather than continuous monitoring, so the exact moment of OOM-kill relative to health checks is inferred from ordering rather than a single atomic trace, though the evidence is internally consistent."]},"ttft_ms":13531,"type":"result","duration_ms":45992,"uuid":"2f20df0c-f353-4dec-8746-9c5820824e06","ttft_stream_ms":736,"time_to_request_ms":42,"first_content_frame_ms":1099,"queued_turn_count":0}
